IQR (Interquartile Range)?
=======

Why Use IQR?

| Advantage                             | Reason                                            |
| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Works well for skewed data            | Not affected much by extreme values               |
| Easy to calculate                     | Uses simple quartiles                             |
| Widely used                           | Common in data preprocessing and machine learning |
| No assumption about data distribution | Works without assuming normality                  |

============

IQR (Interquartile Range) is a statistical method used to detect outliers (unusually high or low values) in numerical data.

What is IQR?

IQR measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data.

Formula:

IQR = Q3 − Q1

Where:

Q1 (First Quartile) = 25th percentile
Q2 (Median) = 50th percentile
Q3 (Third Quartile) = 75th percentile
Example

Suppose the values are:

10, 12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 25, 28, 30
Step 1: Find Quartiles
Quartile	Value
Q1	15
Median (Q2)	20
Q3	28
Step 2: Calculate IQR
IQR = Q3 − Q1

IQR = 28 − 15

IQR = 13
Step 3: Calculate Lower and Upper Bounds

Lower Bound

Q1 − 1.5 × IQR

15 − (1.5 × 13)

15 − 19.5

= -4.5

Upper Bound

Q3 + 1.5 × IQR

28 + 19.5

= 47.5
Step 4: Detect Outliers

Any value

< -4.5

or

> 47.5

is considered an outlier.

